## 2026-08-24 -- tui.App decomposition PR 6/6 (final): extract postingListModel + postingDetailModel (RFC #19)
Context: sixth and final implementation PR against RFC #19 -- the hard
pair the RFC's design work centered on, reaching the RFC's declared end
state. Scope: the posting-list and posting-detail screens.

Decision, postingListModel{store, cursor}: same shape as PR 1's
companyListModel. postings/postingMarkup/hideArchived/activeFilter*
stay App-owned domain data, passed in as a postingListSnapshot on every
call. Four new intent messages (backToCompanyListMsg,
enterPostingDetailMsg, enterFilterSelectMsg, toggleHideArchivedMsg)
cover this screen's cross-screen effects; i/x/o dispatch real async
commands directly via the model's own store reference, same as PR 1's
company list.

Decision, postingDetailModel{store, documents, viewport, posting,
application, hasApplication}: the RFC's harder design, implemented
close to as specified but with one deliberate change made during
implementation -- applicationsByPosting stays App-owned (not moved
into this model), because it's a genuine cross-visit cache the async
loadApplication/applicationLoaded round trip depends on; removing it
would have meant every re-visit to a previously-viewed posting
flashing "no application" until the async refresh resolved. Instead,
App.lookupPosting(id) seeds a fresh postingDetailModel from the map at
every construction point (entering from the list, h/l navigation, and
the four application-mutating *UpdatedMsg handlers reconstructing the
currently-displayed posting after a save) -- postingDetailModel itself
never holds a reference to the map, matching the RFC's "snapshot, not
shared pointer" principle even though the map's contents feed it.
newPostingDetailModel always rebuilds the viewport fresh (matching the
pre-extraction showPostingDetail's "always rebuild, always reset
scroll" behavior) rather than mutating an existing instance in place.

Two real design gaps surfaced only once this was wired into App and
tested against real key sequences, not caught by isolated unit tests
alone:

1. h/l navigation inside detail no longer touches postingList.cursor
   (the two models are genuinely independent, per the isolation goal)
   -- but returning to the list via esc/b used to implicitly preserve
   cursor position for free, since postingCursor was one shared field.
   Fix: postingList.setCursor(i) plus an explicit reconciliation step
   in App's backToPostingListMsg handling
   (a.postingList.setCursor(indexOfPosting(a.postings,
   a.postingDetail.posting.ID))) -- the parent syncs the two models at
   exactly the one transition boundary where it's user-visible, rather
   than either model needing to know about the other. Caught by two
   existing app_test.go tests
   (TestApp_PostingDetail_RightMovesToNextPostingStayingInDetail,
   TestApp_PostingDetail_VimHL_MovesBetweenPostingsLikeArrows) that
   asserted directly on the old shared postingCursor field -- fixing
   them to assert on app.postingDetail.posting.Title instead (the
   actually-observable behavior) surfaced that assumption was baked
   into the old field, not derivable from user-visible behavior alone.

2. Those same two tests' fixture data had backwards title expectations
   once written to check actual posting correspondence rather than raw
   cursor arithmetic: ListPostingsByCompany orders first_seen_at DESC,
   id DESC, so with two postings sharing a first_seen_at (one sync
   batch), the later-inserted one (higher id) sorts first -- the
   original tests never caught this because they only asserted the
   cursor int incremented, never which posting was actually at that
   index. Not a bug this PR introduced; a pre-existing ordering fact
   the old tests happened not to depend on.

resetCursorIfOutOfBounds (distinct from PR 1/3's clampCursor, which
lands on the last valid index) preserves companyFiltersSavedMsg's
original "reset to 0" semantics after a filter re-narrows the list --
named and kept separate rather than reusing clampCursor for a
different behavior at that call site.

Verified: full test suite (62 tui tests -- ~25 postingCursor and ~15
detailViewport call sites renamed to postingList.cursor/
postingDetail.viewport; two tests reworked as described above; no
other behavior changes) plus 17 new isolated tests across
posting_list_test.go and posting_detail_test.go (cursor
movement/clamp/reset, all five intent messages, self-contained i/o
commands, application-guarded a/s/n handling, resize). Manually
verified against the real binary via tmux with 3 seeded postings:
list cursor movement, i/x toggles with immediate archive-hide removal
and A-toggle restore, enter/esc round-trip with the new cursor-sync
fix confirmed working (esc from detail after h/l navigation correctly
lands the list cursor on the last-viewed posting, not a stale one),
h/l navigation across all 3 postings with correct content at each
step, a/s/n sub-screens (status cursor correctly seeded, notes
correctly seeded, both save-and-return-to-detail correctly), f
entering filter-select from the list, window resize while on detail,
and q quit. One finding, confirmed pre-existing on main (not a
regression from this PR, left out of scope): the posting-detail
title line doesn't render in this tmux/terminal setup -- reproduced
identically against a from-main build with the same binary/db/key
sequence before any of PR 1-6's changes existed, so it predates this
entire decomposition effort.

This is the final PR in the RFC #19 series -- App now holds no
per-screen UI-only fields beyond the 7 screen model values themselves;
all UI-only state (cursors, form inputs, textareas, viewports) is
private to its owning screen, and all cross-screen effects flow
through typed messages the parent interprets, closing the
encapsulation gap the RFC set out to close.

## 2026-08-26 -- Agent hand-off mechanism: stage package (#26)
Context: the piece the project's been building toward since the 2026-08-10
v1-scope decision ("resume generation needs good data first") and the
documents (#5) / capabilities-document (#6) work -- a CLI mechanism an
external agent can use to discover interested postings and get everything
it needs to draft a tailored cover letter and resume. Swamp itself never
drafts content or calls an LLM; that stays entirely external, consistent
with #5's "consumed directly by an external agent" framing for the
documents package.

Decision, new `stage` package, not `handoff`: the original PRD draft named
this package `handoff`, describing the interaction between Swamp and the
external agent rather than the responsibility the package owns -- flagged
in PR review as naming smell (meta/architectural language, not domain
vocabulary, unlike `sync`/`documents`/`store`). Renamed to `stage`: the
package stages an application for drafting -- gets it into a
ready-and-waiting state without doing the drafting itself.

Decision, single constructed type mirroring sync.Syncer: `stage.New(store,
documents)` returns one `*Stage` value with `List`/`Prepare` methods, not
loose package-level functions -- same shape as `sync.New(...)` -> `*Syncer`
-> `.SyncAll(...)`. Neither `store` nor `documents` import each other or
gain any new dependency; `stage` is the only package that knows about
both, exactly how `sync` is the only package that knows about both `ashby`
and `store`.

Decision, List (read-only) vs Prepare (mutating) split: continues the
project's existing "no silent DB side effects from a read" principle (see
#5's design discussion). `List` queries interested+non-archived postings
via a new store query (postings JOIN posting_markup JOIN companies LEFT
JOIN applications) and filters out any whose application already has both
documents on disk (checked via `documents.Store.Status`) -- safe to call
as often as needed. `Prepare` is the only mutating entry point: gets or
creates the posting's Application (idempotent, mirrors the TUI's existing
"no-op if already exists" behavior for the `a` key), then calls a new
`documents.Store.EnsureDir` to create the document directory if needed.
`documents.Store.Status` and `ForApplication` stay unchanged and
read-only; `EnsureDir` is additive, not a change to that contract.

Decision, CLI surface: two subcommands added to the existing bare
`os.Args` switch in cmd/swamp/main.go, same pattern as `fetch` (no CLI
framework) -- `stage list` prints List's result as a JSON array, `stage
prepare <posting-id>` prints Prepare's result as a JSON object.

Finding during manual verification: `store.ApplicationStatus` (a Go
int-enum) serialized to JSON as a bare integer (`0`) rather than its DB
string form ("application_started") -- undermines the entire point of
machine-readable output, since an external agent would need to know
Swamp's internal enum ordering. Fixed by adding
`ApplicationStatus.MarshalJSON` (delegates to the existing `String()`),
covered by a new test in application_status_test.go. Caught by actually
running the built binary against a scratch db and reading the JSON output,
not by the unit tests alone -- worth noting as a case where the
"documents"-style manual-verification step earned its keep.

No new DB tables/columns beyond the one new SELECT query. No LLM client,
API key handling, or provider code added anywhere in Swamp. TUI unchanged
-- the existing found/not-found document display already reflects this
mechanism's output with no modification needed. PROFILE_REFERENCE.md
untouched, per #6's existing resolution.

Verified: `go build ./...`, `go test ./...` (all packages green, including
11 new stage package tests and 2 new documents/application_status tests),
`golangci-lint run ./...` (0 issues), `gofmt` check clean. Manually
verified the built binary end-to-end against a scratch sqlite db and
documents directory: list excludes a posting with no posting_markup row
and one not marked interested; prepare creates the application and
directory and is idempotent on a second call (second call's Exists flags
correctly reflect a file written between calls); list excludes a posting
once both documents exist on disk; ApplicationStatus renders as a string
after the MarshalJSON fix.

## 2026-08-30 -- Rename documents package/convention to assets
Context: the on-disk storage location for an application's cover
letter/resume markdown was named "documents" throughout (Go package,
SWAMP_DOCUMENTS_PATH env var, default base directory) since #5. Renamed
to "assets" on request.

Decision: full rename, not just the default folder name -- Go package
`documents` -> `assets` (directory and files renamed to match: assets.go,
assets_test.go), `SWAMP_DOCUMENTS_PATH` -> `SWAMP_ASSETS_PATH`, default
base directory `"documents"` -> `"assets"`. Every call site across
cmd/swamp/main.go, stage, and tui updated (import paths, struct field
names App.assets/postingDetailModel.assets, constructor params). Left a
package named `documents` pointing at an `assets` folder would have been
the exact class of naming inconsistency flagged and fixed once already
this project (`handoff` -> `stage`, see the 2026-08-26 entry) -- not
worth reintroducing here.

Not renamed: the TUI's user-facing "Documents" section label and the
plain English word "documents" used generically in comments/tests to
mean "the cover letter and resume" -- those describe the artifacts
themselves, not the storage mechanism, and weren't part of what was
asked.

The package's exported API (Store, Paths, Doc, Status, ForApplication,
NewStore, EnsureDir) needed no changes -- none of those identifiers
named "documents" in the first place, only the package/env var/folder
name did.

Existing real on-disk content (an application's already-drafted
cover_letter.md/resume.md under the old documents/ directory) was moved
to assets/ as part of this change, not left orphaned.

Verified: `go build ./...`, `go test ./...` (all packages green,
including the renamed `assets` package), `golangci-lint run ./...` (0
issues), `gofmt` check clean. Manually re-ran `swamp stage list` and
`swamp stage prepare` against the real project database via `direnv exec
.` after re-approving the changed .envrc -- confirmed existing
application content survived the directory move and new paths resolve
under assets/ with Exists correctly reported.

## 2026-08-30 -- Correction: revert the documents->assets Go rename, keep only the storage path renamed
Context: the previous entry (same date, "Rename documents package/convention
to assets") over-scoped the request. The actual ask was narrower: only the
on-disk storage path for an application's documents should be named
"assets" -- not the Go package, not SWAMP_DOCUMENTS_PATH, not any struct
field. Reverted all of that: package is `documents` again (directory and
files back to documents/documents.go, documents/documents_test.go), env
var is `SWAMP_DOCUMENTS_PATH` again, struct fields are
App.documents/postingDetailModel.documents/Stage.documents again.

What's actually kept from the previous entry: the default value of
SWAMP_DOCUMENTS_PATH changed from "documents" to "assets"
(cmd/swamp/main.go), and the real on-disk content that was already moved
to assets/<id>/ stays there (that's the correct location now, unrelated
to what the Go package is named).

Lesson: "renamed throughout" in an AskUserQuestion option description is
not the same as confirmed scope -- when a rename touches identifiers
beyond what was literally asked (a filesystem path vs. a Go package/env
var/struct field are different things even if they share a word), that's
worth a separate, explicit confirmation rather than inferring "throughout"
covers all of them from one recommended option.

Verified: `go build ./...`, `go test ./...` (all packages green, package
name back to `documents`), `golangci-lint run ./...` (0 issues), `gofmt`
check clean. Manually re-ran `swamp stage prepare` against the real
project database -- confirmed paths still resolve correctly under
assets/<id>/ with existing content intact.

## 2026-08-30 -- Filter select screen scrolls with cursor on long lists (#14)
Context: filterSelectModel.View() (tui/filter_select.go) rendered its
entire department+location option list unconditionally, with no
windowing against terminal height -- unlike company_list and posting_list,
which both already bound rendering to visible rows via visibleWindow(cursor,
n, listRows). For a company with a long department/location list (e.g. a
real company in the dev db has 18 departments + 28 locations), moving the
cursor past what fit on screen left it off-screen instead of scrolling the
view to follow it.

Decision: extract filterWindow(cursor, deptCount, locCount, rows) as a new
pure, unit-tested helper (tui/filter_select.go, tests in
filter_select_test.go) rather than inlining the windowing math into View().
It wraps the existing visibleWindow over the combined department+location
index space, then splits the resulting [start,end) back into per-group
[start,end) ranges. rows is a budget for option lines only -- a group's
section header ("Department"/"Location") renders "for free" whenever that
group has at least one option in the visible window, and disappears
entirely once the window scrolls past it, so it's never shown orphaned
with no items under it. Matches this project's existing pattern of keeping
windowing math in small tested functions (visibleWindow itself) separate
from View(), which this project's testing decisions explicitly exclude
from automated coverage (see tui package doc comment).

filterSelectModel.View gained a listRows int parameter, matching
company_list/posting_list's existing View(items, listRows) shape; the
call site in App.View passes a.listRows(), same as the other two list
screens.

Verified: go build/test/lint/fmt all clean, including 6 new filterWindow
table-driven test cases (fits everything, zero rows, cursor deep in
locations hiding the department header, cursor in departments hiding the
location header, window straddling both groups, zero departments).
Manually verified against the real project database via tmux (15-row
terminal, cohere's 18 departments + 28 locations): windowed rendering
matches the row budget on first entry, scrolling down through the full
list keeps the cursor visible throughout, the straddle case correctly
shows both headers with partial groups when the window spans the
boundary, the Department header disappears once fully scrolled past, and
both ends clamp correctly (first/last item stays visible without
overscrolling).

## 2026-08-30 -- Greenhouse job board support: client + sync multi-source routing (#30)
Context: step 1 of adding Greenhouse support (issue #30) -- a client
package plus the sync.PostingFetcher decoupling its own doc comment had
been waiting on since the 2026-08-11 decision ("deliberately not built
ahead of having a second real example to design it against"). Step 2 (TUI
add-company source picker, createCompany hardcoding "ashby") is
explicitly out of scope for this pass -- sequenced separately per Bob's
own breakdown of the work.

Decision, verify against real docs and real live data before designing:
fetched docs.greenhouse.io directly rather than working from memory, then
probed several real companies' live Greenhouse boards (gitlab, discord,
airbnb) rather than trusting the docs alone. This caught a real bug the
docs didn't mention: Greenhouse's `content` field is HTML-entity-*double*-
encoded -- its JSON string value is itself entity-escaped markup (e.g.
"&lt;p&gt;" not "<p>"). Missing this would have meant DescriptionHTML
wasn't actually renderable HTML and DescriptionText showed raw tag syntax
as visible text instead of stripped plain text. Caught by updating the
test fixture to match the real encoding (turning a passing-by-coincidence
test into a real red), not by the live-data check alone -- the fixture
now guards against regressing this specifically.

Decision, three real design points where Greenhouse's shape doesn't map
cleanly onto Ashby's (confirmed against docs.greenhouse.io/job-board.html,
not guessed): (1) a Greenhouse job can belong to multiple departments
(array), joined into one "; "-separated string since store/filter expect
a single value per posting -- Ashby postings are single-department today
so this doesn't change existing behavior; (2) DescriptionText is derived
via a real HTML tokenizer (golang.org/x/net/html, already in the module
graph as an indirect dep, promoted to direct rather than adding a new
external dependency) rather than left empty, since it's what stage.List
surfaces to an external agent for drafting -- a naive regex stripper was
rejected because it gets entity decoding and <script>/<style> exclusion
wrong; (3) EmploymentType/WorkplaceType are left empty for Greenhouse
postings -- Greenhouse's job board API doesn't expose them in a
structured, cross-company-reliable way (only via per-company-configurable
metadata).

Decision, sync.Posting + per-source adapters over a shared interface on
the client packages: sync gets its own source-agnostic Posting type
(identical shape to what ashby.Posting already was). Each source package
(ashby, greenhouse) keeps its own native Posting type matching what that
source's API actually returns -- greenhouse.Posting has no Team/
EmploymentType/WorkplaceType fields at all, rather than padding it with
fields that source can never populate. AshbyFetcher/GreenhouseFetcher are
thin adapters translating each into sync.Posting and satisfying
PostingFetcher. Syncer holds map[string]PostingFetcher keyed by
store.Company.Source instead of one fetcher; SyncCompany looks up the
configured source and errors on an unconfigured one, same failure mode as
the old ashby-only check. Adding a third source (e.g. #31's Lever) is one
more map entry in cmd/swamp/main.go's newSyncer, no interface changes.

Verified: go build/test/lint/fmt all clean across the whole repo,
including greenhouse's tests (client + stripHTML, both against
fixtures) and sync's tests (existing SyncCompany/SyncAll suite migrated
to the new Posting type and map-based New, plus new AshbyFetcher/
GreenhouseFetcher translation tests). Manually verified against gitlab's
real, live Greenhouse board through the actual client (not just the
fixture): 220 real postings, description HTML/text correctly decoded and
stripped. Also manually verified the full sync pipeline end to end
through the built CLI binary against a scratch db: inserted a
source="greenhouse" company pointed at gitlab's real board, ran `swamp
fetch`, confirmed 220 postings created with sane title/department/
location, and confirmed a second fetch was idempotent (0 created).

## 2026-08-30 -- Company form source picker: step 2 of Greenhouse support (#30)
Context: step 2 of #30 -- letting a user actually add a Greenhouse company
through the TUI, not just via direct SQL. Branched off greenhouse-job-
board-support's tip (not main) since it depends on that PR's sync/
createCompany plumbing.

Decision: source selection is a third field in the existing add-company
form (Tab-cycled alongside Name/SourceRef), not a separate screen.
Picking a source and filling in the board slug/token felt like one
coherent action, not two -- a dedicated screen-then-screen flow would add
a navigation round-trip for what's fundamentally still "fill out one
form." companySources ([]string{"ashby", "greenhouse"}) and
sourceRefLabels (which label the second field gets, since "Ashby slug"
and "Greenhouse board token" mean different things) live in
company_form.go itself, not derived from sync's fetcher map -- there's no
existing canonical exported list of supported sources to derive from
without inventing new cross-package plumbing for two hardcoded strings;
a comment flags that this list must be kept in sync with
cmd/swamp/main.go's newSyncer by hand.

Decision, form layout: kept `inputs []textinput.Model` sized
formFieldCount (3) with formFieldSource's slot simply unused as a
textinput, rather than restructuring around 2 named fields -- this let
the existing formFieldName/formFieldSourceRef constants and every
existing test assertion that references them by name keep working
unchanged (only their integer values shifted, invisibly, since nothing
hardcoded the literal 0/1). Source cycles via left/right arrow only when
it's the focused field -- guarded explicitly so arrow keys don't leak
into the source picker while a text field is focused (a textinput
consumes arrow keys itself, for cursor movement within the field).

Focus starts on the source field (not Name, as it did before) --
picking/confirming a source is now the natural first step of the flow,
and the picker's current selection is always visible in View() regardless
of focus, so nothing is hidden by starting there.

createCompany gained a source parameter (previously hardcoded "ashby");
its one call site in companyFormModel.Update now passes
companySources[m.sourceIndex].

Verified: go build/test/lint/fmt all clean, including 8 new
company_form tests (source-field-first focus, 3-field tab cycling,
left/right cycling on and off the source field, and an end-to-end test
using a real store confirming the selected source actually lands in the
created company row) and 3 existing App-level integration tests updated
for the new field order (they previously assumed focus started on Name).
Manually verified against the real built binary via tmux: opened the
form, confirmed the source picker renders with ashby boxed by default
and the second field labeled "Ashby slug", toggled to greenhouse and
confirmed the label switched to "Greenhouse board token", submitted a
real Greenhouse company (GitLab, board token "gitlab") and confirmed it
stored with source=greenhouse, then pressed 'r' to refresh and confirmed
it synced for real against GitLab's live Greenhouse board (220 postings
created) -- proving the full chain (TUI form -> store -> sync routing ->
GreenhouseFetcher -> real API) works end to end together. Also verified
submitting with the default (ashby) source unchanged, no regression.

## 2026-08-30 -- Edit an existing company's name (#35)
Context: #35, narrowed during scoping discussion to name-only editing --
source/source_ref are locked at creation and have no edit path at all,
since changing either really means "this is a different board," not
"edit this company," and already-ingested postings.source/source_id
values wouldn't retroactively reflect a change anyway (see the issue's
"Out of scope" section, and #36 for the separate create-time validation
idea that came out of the same discussion).

Decision: a new, separate companyEditModel (tui/company_edit.go) rather
than adding an edit mode to companyFormModel. Mirrors applicationNotesModel's
shape (store + target ID + single focused input, esc cancels, a save key
dispatches a command) more closely than companyFormModel's multi-field
Tab-cycling shape -- editing is a single field, so there's no field-cycling
or source-picker concern to inherit. store gained UpdateCompanyName
(new sqlc query, same deleted_at IS NULL guard as GetCompany -- editing a
soft-deleted company isn't supported, matching how it's already invisible
everywhere else).

New 'e' keybinding on the company list, alongside the existing a/d/r.
Wired the same way enterApplicationNotesMsg/applicationNotesUpdatedMsg
already are: an intent type (enterCompanyEditMsg) defined in
company_list.go where it's emitted, a command + result msg
(updateCompanyName/companyNameUpdatedMsg) defined in app.go alongside
createCompany/deleteCompany, and the result handler updates a.companies
in place by ID (same pattern as companyDeletedMsg, but replacing instead
of removing).

Verified: go build/test/lint/fmt all clean, including 3 new store tests
(name changes, unchanged Source/SourceRef, ErrNotFound for a nonexistent
or soft-deleted company), 5 new companyEditModel tests, 2 new
companyListModel tests for the 'e' key, and one new App-level integration
test covering the full flow (press e, confirm the field is seeded from
the selected company, edit, submit, confirm both the in-memory list and
the stored row reflect the new name while ID/Source/SourceRef stay
unchanged). Manually verified against the real built binary via tmux:
added a company, pressed e, confirmed the name field is pre-seeded,
appended text and saved, confirmed the list shows the updated name and
the DB row kept the same id/source/source_ref, and confirmed esc after
typing cancels without saving.

## 2026-08-31 -- Move project instructions to AGENTS.md, CLAUDE.md imports it
Context: the repo's instructions file (formerly CLAUDE.md) had no Claude-
specific content in it -- it was pure repo/engineering workflow guidance
(issue workflow, testing, branching, tooling, decisions log) that applies
identically to any coding agent working in this codebase. AGENTS.md is
the emerging cross-tool convention for exactly this.

Decision: AGENTS.md is now the source of truth (verbatim content, moved
wholesale, only the intro line reworded to be tool-agnostic). CLAUDE.md
is a real, small file (not a symlink) containing `@AGENTS.md` -- Claude
Code's documented import syntax -- so Claude Code's own auto-discovery
(which looks for CLAUDE.md specifically, confirmed it has no first-class
AGENTS.md support) still picks up the same content, with room to add
genuinely Claude-Code-specific instructions below the import later if any
ever arise. A symlink (`ln -s AGENTS.md CLAUDE.md`) was considered and
rejected in favor of two real files -- explicit preference, not a
technical constraint (the symlink is verified to work fine on macOS/
Linux; Windows needs Admin/Developer Mode for it, which the import
syntax sidesteps entirely, but that wasn't the deciding factor here).

Also folded in a process fix while touching this file: added an explicit
"use GitHub's closing-keyword syntax (Fixes #N), not a bare #N reference"
line to the feature-branch workflow section, from the #35-stayed-open-
after-#42-merged incident earlier the same day.

Verified: both files read back correctly, diffed AGENTS.md's body against
the prior CLAUDE.md to confirm only the two intended changes (reworded
intro line, new closing-keyword item) -- no accidental content loss in
the move.

## 2026-08-31 -- apply-to-posting skill moved to .agents/skills/, symlinked from .claude/skills/
Context: continuing the AGENTS.md migration's spirit (tool-agnostic
canonical location, not buried under a tool-specific dotdir) for the
apply-to-posting skill, which already had no Claude-specific wording in
its body.

Unlike CLAUDE.md, Claude Code has no import syntax for skills -- verified
via claude-code-guide against the real docs. Skills are only discovered
at `.claude/skills/<name>/SKILL.md`, hard-coded, no settings.json search
path option. The only supported way to keep one canonical copy outside
that dir is a symlink; Claude Code explicitly documents following
symlinked skill directories.

Directory name went through a correction: first landed on `agent/skills/`
(matching the singular, un-dotted feel of `AGENTS.md`), but Bob flagged
`.agents/skills/` as the standard he'd seen. Checked both claims before
picking: web search says `.agents/skills/` is a widely-cited convention
tied to the open Agent Skills format spec at agentskills.io; a direct
doc check via claude-code-guide found Claude Code's own docs mention only
`.claude/skills/` (personal/project), enterprise-managed, and plugin
paths -- no `.agents/skills/` anywhere. The two aren't actually in
conflict: agentskills.io standardizes the SKILL.md file format, not a
discovery path -- each tool picks its own path independently, and
Claude Code hasn't adopted `.agents/skills/` as one. So naming it
`.agents/skills/` doesn't buy native discovery either way; it was Bob's
explicit call once that was laid out, made for consistency with the
convention he's seen elsewhere.

Decision: canonical file is now `.agents/skills/apply-to-posting/
SKILL.md` (moved with `git mv` to preserve history, after first moving
through `agent/skills/`). `.claude/skills/apply-to-posting` is a symlink
to `../../.agents/skills/apply-to-posting`. This is the opposite call
from the CLAUDE.md/AGENTS.md split (real file there, symlink rejected)
-- different situation, since there's no lightweight import mechanism
for a multi-file skill the way `@AGENTS.md` covers a single small file.

Verified: symlink resolves (`ls -la .claude/skills/` shows it pointing at
`../../.agents/skills/apply-to-posting`, `readlink` confirms, `SKILL.md`
readable through it), `git status` shows a rename for the skill file
(history preserved, not add+delete), grepped AGENTS.md/README.md for
stale `.claude/skills`, `agent/skills`, or `SKILL.md` path references --
none found.

## 2026-08-31 -- Posting list columns aligned with lipgloss/table (#46)
Problem: `postingListModel.View` built each row with `fmt.Sprintf("%s %s |
%s | %s | %s", ...)` -- no fixed column widths, so the `|` separators
landed at a different horizontal position on every line. `github.com/
charmbracelet/lipgloss/table` was already sitting in the dependency graph
(a subpackage of lipgloss v1.1.0, already a direct dependency) and does
column width computation natively.

Decision: adopted `lipgloss/table` with its default bordered rendering --
Bob's comment on the issue confirmed the table doesn't need to be
borderless or replicate the old minimal look, so no custom border
stripping was needed. Columns: marker (★/✕/blank), Title, Department,
Location, Status, with a header row. Cell values are truncated to a
fixed max width per column (title 40, department 18, location 20,
status 8) with an ellipsis via `github.com/charmbracelet/x/ansi`'s
`TruncateWc` (already an indirect dependency, pulled in by lipgloss/
table itself -- promoted to direct with `go mod tidy`) before being
handed to the table, rather than relying on the table's own wrap
behavior.

This was the real decision point the issue flagged: every list screen in
this TUI (`visibleWindow` in `viewport.go`) assumes exactly one physical
line per item for its cursor/scroll math. Letting cells wrap would have
broken that assumption everywhere it's used -- truncating keeps every
row exactly one line, which is why cells are pre-truncated (width-aware,
not byte-aware, so multi-byte runes truncate correctly) rather than
handed to the table untouched and left to `lipgloss/table`'s own
wrapping.

The selected row is no longer marked with a `"> "` text prefix (awkward
inside table cells) -- instead the whole row is styled with the existing
`cursorStyle` (foreground 212) via the table's `StyleFunc`, keeping the
same visual language as the rest of the app.

The table's own border/header adds 4 physical lines beyond one per data
row (top border, header, header separator, bottom border) that aren't
accounted for in `chromeRows` (a fixed constant shared across all list
screens). Rather than touching the shared constant, `postingListModel.
View` subtracts a local `postingTableChromeLines = 4` from `listRows`
before computing `visibleWindow`'s row budget, scoped to this screen
only.

Scope: `company_list.go`'s `"%s (%s)"` rows are a label, not a table
(matches the issue's own scoping note) -- left untouched.

Verified: `TestTruncateCol` (TDD, table-driven) covers under/over/equal-
to-max and a wide-rune (CJK) case -- confirmed the expected truncated
value against `ansi.TruncateWc`'s actual output rather than assuming it,
and asserted `lipgloss.Width` of the result never exceeds max. Manually
verified against a real production dataset (913 real postings across 10
companies, copied to a scratch db) via tmux: confirmed columns align
across rows of varying length, long titles truncate with an ellipsis,
a real double-width Japanese title (`シニアコンサルティングエンジニア`)
renders and aligns correctly, cursor row highlighting still works and
coexists with the marker column, and a 41-row unfiltered table (Clickhouse)
renders within the terminal height with no overflow -- confirming the
`postingTableChromeLines` budget subtraction is correct.

## 2026-08-31 -- Four bug-labeled issues from the #35 code review (#38, #39, #41, #37)
All four were found during code review of #35 and filed as separate
issues; tackled together in one branch/PR since they're small, related,
and touch adjacent code, rather than one branch each.

**#38 -- whitespace-only company name accepted.** Both `companyFormModel`
and `companyEditModel` guarded against an empty name with `name == ""`
but never trimmed first, so `"   "` passed the check and saved a blank-
looking company. Fixed by trimming before the check in both places, and
saving the trimmed value (not just checking it) so `"  Acme  "` doesn't
persist with stray whitespace either. No downstream validation exists
(`updateCompanyName`, `store.UpdateCompanyName`, the SQL) -- the TUI
guard is the only place this can reasonably be caught.

**#39 -- company list goes out of alphabetical order after create/
rename.** `ListActiveCompanies` orders by name, but `a.companies` was
mutated in place (append on create, in-place replace on rename) with
nothing re-sorting afterward. Chose to re-sort client-side
(`sortCompaniesByName`, using Go's `<` on `Name`, which matches SQLite's
default `BINARY` collation for ASCII) rather than reloading the full list
from the store on every mutation -- smaller change, keeps the existing
synchronous update semantics the #35 test (`TestApp_PressE_
EditsCompanyName`) already depends on.

**#41 -- cancelling a screen never clears the stale error banner.**
`a.err` was only ever overwritten by the next async result, so an error
from a failed action, followed by the user cancelling out rather than
retrying, kept showing indefinitely on whatever screen they landed on
next. Fixed systemically per the issue's own suggestion, not per cancel
handler: `App.Update`'s `tea.KeyMsg` case now delegates to a new
`updateKeyMsg` method and clears `a.err` whenever that call actually
changes `a.screen` -- covers all screen transitions (cancel-out and
forward navigation alike), not just the five `cancelXMsg` handlers named
in the issue.

**#37 -- Esc doesn't cancel an in-flight company-name save.** This one
was `TBD` in the issue -- needed a decision, not just a fix. Options
laid out were: block Esc while a save is pending, queue Esc and apply it
once the save resolves, or accept the race and just fix the misleading
"without saving" wording. Bob's call, after discussion: block. The
queuing option turned out to have a real problem once worked through --
on save success the screen already auto-navigates away on its own
(nothing left for a queued Esc to do), and on save failure a queued Esc
would have to auto-navigate away too, which means silently discarding a
failed save's error instead of showing it (the "silent fallback"
anti-pattern). Blocking sidesteps both: `companyEditModel` gained a
`saving bool`, set on Enter and cleared by a new `saveResolved()` method
that `App` calls from the `companyNameUpdatedMsg` handler regardless of
success or failure. Esc is a no-op while `saving` is true; once it
clears, either the screen has already left (success) or the user is
still there with the error visible and Esc now works normally (failure).

Verified: `TestCompanyEditModel_Esc_WhileSavePending_NoOp` and
`_AfterSaveResolved_ReturnsCancelMsg` (model-level), plus
`TestApp_EscWhileCompanyNameSavePending_DoesNotRaceTheSave` (App-level,
reproduces the exact race: dispatches the save command without running
it, sends Esc, confirms the screen didn't move, then runs the command
and confirms the save landed). Confirmed each new test actually catches
the regression it targets by stashing the corresponding fix and
re-running -- #41's test fails with the stale error still present;
#37's test fails to even compile without `saveResolved` (a stronger
signal than an assertion failure). #38/#39 got the same TDD red-first
treatment inline rather than a stash check, since their fixes are single-
line and the failure mode is obvious from the assertion message.
Manually verified #38/#39 together against a real production dataset via
tmux: creating "  Zeta Corp  " saved as "Zeta Corp", inserted in the
correct alphabetical position (`BINARY` collation: uppercase before
lowercase) between the existing "Wealthsimple" and "clickhouse"; a
whitespace-only name was correctly rejected as a no-op; normal (non-
racing) edit-cancel and edit-save both still work with real save timing.
